<!-- #region vectors -->

| Operation | Working | Result |
|---|---|---|
| u + w | (1 + 4, 2 + 5, 3 + 6) | (5, 7, 9) |
| w − u | (4 − 1, 5 − 2, 6 − 3) | (3, 3, 3) |
| 3u (scalar multiple) | (3·1, 3·2, 3·3) | (3, 6, 9) |
| u · w (dot product) | 1·4 + 2·5 + 3·6 = 4 + 10 + 18 | **32** (a scalar) |
| u × w (cross product) | (2·6 − 3·5, 3·4 − 1·6, 1·5 − 2·4) | **(−3, 6, −3)** (a vector) |
| ‖u‖ (length) | √(1² + 2² + 3²) = √14 | 3.7417 |

Check the cross product: (u × w) · u = 0 and (u × w) · w = 0. Both are 0, so u × w is perpendicular to both, as it must be. These are the same vectors as Assignment 1 Q10, where `np.dot(u, w)` printed 32 and `np.cross(u, w)` printed [−3, 6, −3].

<!-- #endregion vectors -->
<!-- #region matmul -->

$$ \underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\ 4 & 5 & 6\end{bmatrix}}_{2\times 3}\;\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\ 2 & 1 \\ 0 & 3\end{bmatrix}}_{3\times 2} = \begin{bmatrix} 1\cdot1+2\cdot2+3\cdot0 & 1\cdot0+2\cdot1+3\cdot3 \\ 4\cdot1+5\cdot2+6\cdot0 & 4\cdot0+5\cdot1+6\cdot3 \end{bmatrix} = \underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}5 & 11 \\ 14 & 23\end{bmatrix}}_{2\times 2} $$

Entry (i, j) is row i of the first matrix **dotted with** column j of the second. Shapes: (2 × **3**)(**3** × 2) works because the inner numbers match, and the answer takes the outer numbers, 2 × 2. In the other order, BA is (3 × 2)(2 × 3) = 3 × 3: a different size altogether, so **AB ≠ BA** (matrix multiplication is not commutative).

<!-- #endregion matmul -->
<!-- #region eigen -->

Take $M = \begin{bmatrix} 4 & 1 \\ 2 & 3 \end{bmatrix}$.

$$ \begin{aligned} \det M &= ad - bc = 4\cdot3 - 1\cdot2 = 10 \\ M^{-1} &= \frac{1}{\det M}\begin{bmatrix} d & -b \\ -c & a \end{bmatrix} = \frac{1}{10}\begin{bmatrix} 3 & -1 \\ -2 & 4 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} \tfrac{3}{10} & -\tfrac{1}{10} \\ -\tfrac{1}{5} & \tfrac{2}{5} \end{bmatrix} \\ \det(M - \lambda I) &= (4-\lambda)(3-\lambda) - 2 = \lambda^2 - 7\lambda + 10 = (\lambda - 5)(\lambda - 2) = 0 \\ \lambda_1 &= 5, \quad \lambda_2 = 2 \end{aligned} $$

**Eigenvectors.** For λ = 5: (M − 5I)v = 0 gives −v₁ + v₂ = 0, so v = (1, 1). For λ = 2: 2v₁ + v₂ = 0, so v = (1, −2). Check: M(1, 1) = (5, 5) = 5 × (1, 1). Two shortcuts that catch mistakes: λ₁ + λ₂ = trace = 4 + 3 = 7, and λ₁ × λ₂ = det = 10.

<!-- #endregion eigen -->
<!-- #region pearson -->

Hours studied (x) and marks (y) for five students. Means: x̄ = 3, ȳ = 60.

| x | y | x − x̄ | y − ȳ | (x − x̄)(y − ȳ) | (x − x̄)² | (y − ȳ)² |
|:-:|:-:|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|
| 1 | 52 | −2 | −8 | 16 | 4 | 64 |
| 2 | 55 | −1 | −5 | 5 | 1 | 25 |
| 3 | 61 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 1 |
| 4 | 60 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 1 | 0 |
| 5 | 72 | 2 | 12 | 24 | 4 | 144 |
| **Σ 15** | **Σ 300** | 0 | 0 | **45** | **10** | **234** |

$$ r = \frac{45}{\sqrt{10 \times 234}} = \frac{45}{48.3735} = \mathbf{0.9303} $$

**r = 0.93: a strong positive linear relationship.** More hours go with more marks, though not perfectly (the fourth student studied more and scored less). The sample covariance on the way there is Σ(x − x̄)(y − ȳ)/(n − 1) = 45/4 = 11.25: its sign gives the direction, but its size depends on the units, which is why r divides it out.

<!-- #endregion pearson -->
